<Base
  title="About — Tomoki Cheung"
  description="Computer science student in Honolulu, running a small web studio and exploring where software and hardware meet."
>
  <p class="meta">About</p>
  <h1>Tomoki Cheung</h1>

  <div class="prose body">
    <p>
      I am studying Computer Science at the University of Hawai&#699;i at
      M&#257;noa on the general track, with an AS in Information Technology from
      Kapi&#699;olani Community College. Alongside school, I run a small web
      development studio in Honolulu and build sites for businesses that do not
      have anyone technical on staff.
    </p>

    <p>
      I am interested in eventually working somewhere between hardware and
      software. So far, most of what I have actually built has been on the
      software side. Reading about hardware is not the same as building with it,
      so the next step is to buy parts and start finding out where my
      understanding breaks down. I have considered starting with a Raspberry Pi
      or taking an off-the-shelf robot and trying to make it do something beyond
      what it was originally set up to do.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>

    <p>
      I use large language models heavily when I work, and over the past year
      they have let me attempt things earlier than I otherwise could have. That
      also creates a different problem: it is possible to make something work
      before fully understanding why it works.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because of that, I try to verify what I am given instead of accepting it at
      face value. I check for exposed credentials before commits. If I add a
      safeguard, I try to make it fail so I know it is actually doing something.
      I keep a written record of mistakes and how they were found, although I
      have learned that writing a problem down and actually fixing it are two
      different things.
    </p>

    <p>
      When a model tells me something works, I want to understand why. I would
      rather discover early that I misunderstood something than ship something I
      cannot explain. I am still learning where these tools help me move faster
      and where using them too quickly can hide gaps in what I know.
    </p>

    <h2>Background</h2>

    <p>
      I was born in Hawai&#699;i to immigrant parents. My father, his younger
      brother, his parents, and several relatives on his side came to
      Hawai&#699;i from China without speaking English and with limited
      resources. My mother comes from a large family in Hokkaido. Her side
      rarely visits Hawai&#699;i, so my mother, my brother, and I have usually
      been the ones traveling to Japan to see them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Growing up between those two sides of my family gave me a somewhat unusual
      upbringing. A lot of my perspective comes from moving between different
      places, families, and ways of doing things.
    </p>

    <h2>Away from the desk</h2>

    <p>
      I have been to a handful of prefectures in Japan, but Hokkaido is the one
      that feels most familiar. Sapporo is usually where I stay, and Hakodate is
      somewhere I would happily spend more time. Our honke, the main family
      household, is in Esashi in the northeast. Getting there from Sapporo takes
      most of a day in practice because we usually stop to visit relatives along
      the way. The family traces back to Fukui before Hokkaido.
    </p>

    <p>
      Kyoto is the other place I keep returning to. There is something about it
      I have never been able to explain particularly well. Even with the crowds,
      I still find parts of it unusually quiet.
    </p>

    <p>
      My favorite game is Detroit: Become Human. I do not expect its version of
      androids or artificial intelligence to become reality as the game presents
      them. What I like is the question underneath the fiction: what happens when
      a technology develops faster than people decide what role it should have?
      The game pushes that idea far beyond where current technology is, but the
      question itself is part of why I am interested in AI, robotics, and the
      relationship between software and the physical world.
    </p>
  </div>

